If JSON body parsing is introduced, set an explicit limit:

```js
app.use(express.json({ limit: '100kb' }));
```

For URL-encoded forms:

```js
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false, limit: '100kb' }));
```

---

## 8. No CORS policy defined

### Severity: Depends on deployment

The app currently does not enable CORS, which means browsers will block cross-origin reads by default. That is safer than permissive CORS.

However, if CORS is added later using a permissive configuration like this:

```js
app.use(cors());
```

then any origin could call the API from a browser.

### Recommendation

If browser-based clients need access, define an explicit allowlist.

Install:

```bash
npm install cors
```

Example:

```js
const cors = require('cors');

const allowedOrigins = [
  'https://example.com',
  'https://admin.example.com',
];

app.use(cors({
  origin(origin, callback) {
    if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {
      return callback(null, true);
    }

    return callback(new Error('Not allowed by CORS'));
  },
  methods: ['GET'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization', 'x-api-key'],
}));
```

Do not combine `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` with credentials.

---

## 9. No centralized error handling

### Severity: Low currently

There is no custom error handler. The current routes are simple and unlikely to throw, but real applications should avoid leaking stack traces or internal details in responses.

### Recommendation

Add a not-found handler and centralized error handler.

```js
app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' });
});

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);

  res.status(500).json({
    error: 'Internal server error',
  });
});
```

Avoid returning `err.stack`, raw exception messages, SQL errors, or filesystem paths to clients.

---

## 10. Server starts automatically when the module is imported

### Severity: Low

This line runs the server immediately:

```js
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
```

Because `app` is also exported:

```js
module.exports = app;
```

this can cause side effects during tests or when another module imports the app.

### Recommendation

Only listen when the file is executed directly:

```js
if (require.main === module) {
  const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;

  app.listen(port, () => {
    console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);
  });
}

module.exports = app;
```

This improves testability and avoids accidental server startup.

---

## 11. Hardcoded port

### Severity: Low

The server always binds to port `3000`.

```js
app.listen(3000, ...)
```

This is inflexible for production, containers, and cloud deployment platforms.

### Recommendation

Use an environment variable:

```js
const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
```

---

## 12. No HTTPS enforcement

### Severity: Medium in production

The app itself does not enforce HTTPS. Usually TLS is terminated at a reverse proxy or load balancer, but the application should still be deployed behind HTTPS.

### Recommendation

If behind a proxy, configure Express trust proxy correctly and redirect HTTP to HTTPS where appropriate.

```js
app.set('trust proxy', 1);

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production' && !req.secure) {
    return res.redirect(301, `https://${req.headers.host}${req.originalUrl}`);
  }

  next();
});
```

Only use this if your proxy correctly sets `X-Forwarded-Proto`.

