### Severity: Low

```js
app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok', timestamp: new Date().toISOString() });
});
```

### Risk

A timestamp is not usually sensitive, but it can help fingerprint uptime or deployment behavior. This is minor.

### Recommendation

For public health checks, return minimal information.

```js
app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok' });
});
```

For internal observability, expose detailed diagnostics only on protected internal routes.

---

## 8. Hardcoded Port

### Severity: Low

```js
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
```

### Risk

Hardcoded ports reduce deployment flexibility and can cause misconfigurations.

### Recommendation

Use an environment variable.

```js
const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);
});
```

---

## 9. App Starts Listening During Import

### Severity: Low

The file both exports `app` and starts the server:

```js
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});

module.exports = app;
```

### Risk

This complicates testing and can cause unintended network listeners when the app is imported by another module or test suite.

### Recommendation

Only listen when the file is run directly.

```js
if (require.main === module) {
  const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;

  app.listen(port, () => {
    console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);
  });
}

module.exports = app;
```

---

## 10. No Explicit JSON Body Size Limit

### Severity: Low currently

The app does not parse request bodies today. If body parsing is added later with `express.json()`, it should include a size limit.

### Risk

Large request bodies can cause memory pressure or denial of service.

### Recommendation

If JSON parsing is needed:

```js
app.use(express.json({ limit: '100kb' }));
```

---

## 11. CORS Not Configured

### Severity: Context-dependent

The app does not configure CORS. That is not automatically a vulnerability. In fact, the absence of permissive CORS is usually safer.

### Risk

If CORS is added later with a wildcard, browsers may allow unintended cross-origin access.

Bad:

```js
app.use(cors({
  origin: '*',
  credentials: true,
}));
```

This is invalid and dangerous when credentials are involved.

### Recommendation

Only allow trusted origins.

```js
const cors = require('cors');

const allowedOrigins = [
  'https://example.com',
  'https://admin.example.com',
];

app.use(cors({
  origin(origin, callback) {
    if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {
      return callback(null, true);
    }

    return callback(new Error('Not allowed by CORS'));
  },
  credentials: true,
}));
```

For server-to-server APIs, CORS may not be needed at all.

